def SP(name,slug): return ['Hawaii State Parks: '+name,DSP+slug+'/']
KAPU=' Heiau are sacred places to Hawaiians: stay on the paths, do not climb the walls, move stones or leave wrapped offerings.'
PLACES=[
N('puu-o-mahuka-heiau','Puʻu o Mahuka Heiau','Haleiwa','hi','burial','ruins',21.641446,-158.059037,'site','public','drive','','1600s–1819',
  'The largest heiau (temple) on Oahu, on a bluff above Waimea Bay and Waimea Valley. State Parks says it covers almost 2 acres and may have been built in the 1600s; Wikipedia says the upper enclosure may date from the 17th century and the two lower ones from the 18th. In the 1770s it was probably a luakini (sacrificial temple); after Kamehameha I took Oahu in 1795 his high priest Hewahewa held ceremonies here, and it stayed in use until the kapu system was abolished in 1819. It became a state park and a National Historic Landmark in 1962.',
  'Three walled enclosures of stacked stone, the walls 3 to 6 feet high, with paved floors. The upper, eastern enclosure was the first built.',
  'The biggest temple platform on Oahu, looking down on Waimea Bay.',
  'Hawaii Division of State Parks (Puʻu o Mahuka Heiau State Historic Site): open daily 9:00 am to 5:30 pm, no entrance fee. Reached by Pupukea Road (Route 835), which leaves Kamehameha Highway opposite the Pupukea fire station; there is a small lot at the site.'+KAPU,
  [SP('Puʻu o Mahuka Heiau State Historic Site','puu-o-mahuka-heiau-state-historic-site'),W('Puʻu o Mahuka Heiau State Monument')],
  conf='mixed',th=[21.64160,-158.05851],thn='Lot at the end of Puʻu o Mahuka Road'),
N('keaiwa-heiau','Keaīwa Heiau','Aiea','hi','burial','ruins',21.39655,-157.90490,'site','public','drive','','1500s?',
  'A heiau hoʻola, a healing temple, where a kahuna treated the sick and taught students to heal with plants, fasting and prayer. State Parks says it is not known when it was built; one source says the 1500s, by the Oahu chief Kakuhihewa and his kahuna Keaiwa. The forest around it was replanted in the late 1920s.',
  'A stacked rock wall about 4 feet high enclosing an area of about 100 by 160 feet, at the entrance of the park. The 4.8-mile ʻAiea Loop Trail starts further up the park road; State Parks notes that remnants of a B-24 bomber that crashed in 1944 can be seen along it.',
  'A healing temple at the gate of a forest park above Pearl Harbor.',
  'Keaīwa Heiau State Recreation Area, Hawaii Division of State Parks: open 7:00 am to 6:45 pm in winter (from the day after Labor Day) and to 7:45 pm in summer (from April 1); no entrance fee. Dogs are not allowed in the park except leashed on the way to the ʻAiea Loop Trail. Drive up ʻAiea Heights Drive to the park gate.'+KAPU,
  [SP('Keaīwa Heiau State Recreation Area','keaiwa-heiau-state-recreation-area')],
  th=[21.39603,-157.90175],thn='Lot on the park road, Keaīwa Heiau State Recreation Area'),
N('ulupo-heiau','Ulupō Heiau','Kailua','hi','burial','ruins',21.385636,-157.752876,'site','public','drive','','to about 1780s',
  'A massive stone temple platform on the east edge of Kawai Nui Marsh, linked in legend with the menehune and later with the Oahu high chiefs Kakuhihewa and Kualiʻi. Wikipedia says it may have peaked around 1750 and was abandoned after Oahu was conquered in the 1780s. It became a territorial park in 1954, was partly restored in the early 1960s, and got its bronze plaque from the Commission on Historical Sites in 1962.',
  'The great terraced stone platform, with notches in the terraces where wooden images once stood (State Parks), and the plaque. Kawai Nui, once a 400-acre fishpond, lies below.',
  '',
  'Ulupō Heiau State Historic Site, Hawaii Division of State Parks, beside the Windward YMCA. State Parks gives the park hours as Monday to Friday 7:00 am to 7:00 pm, matched to the YMCA, with the gate open Monday to Friday 8:00 am to noon; when the gate is closed, park in the YMCA lot next door and walk to the heiau. No entrance fee. Weekend opening was not stated.'+KAPU,
  [SP('Ulupō Heiau State Historic Site','ulupo-heiau-state-historic-site'),W('Ulupō Heiau State Historic Site')]),
N('kukaniloko-birthstones','Kūkaniloko Birthstones','Wahiawa','hi','burial','traces',21.504725,-158.036218,'site','view','drive','','',
  'One of the most sacred places on Oahu: chiefly children were born here, at the meeting of the old Waialua trail and the Kolekole trail across the plateau. The Daughters of Hawaii recognised and protected the stones in 1925; the associated Hoʻolonopahu Heiau was destroyed to make way for sugar and pineapple fields. A University of Hawaii astronomy team recorded designs on the stones in 2000 that may have been used to track the sky. The Office of Hawaiian Affairs took on 511 acres around the site in 2012 as a buffer.',
  'A grove with a group of boulders in an open field; Wikipedia counts 36 stones in the complex.',
  '',
  'Closed to the public. The site\'s stewards, the Hawaiian Civic Club of Wahiawā (kukaniloko.org), say it remains closed with no escorted visits; the Office of Hawaiian Affairs, which manages the land around it, says it was closed out of precaution in the Covid years and refers questions to the stewards. A travel article (Islands, read in this session) says public access was removed in January 2022 but the stones can still be seen from the junction of Whitmore Avenue and Kamehameha Highway (Route 80), with no parking area there. Do not enter.',
  [['Islands: Kūkaniloko Birthstones','https://www.islands.com/1746195/kukaniloko-birthstones-sacred-site-hawaii-oahu-hawaii-shares-timeless-tradition-stunning-views'],['Hawaiian Civic Club of Wahiawā: Kūkaniloko','https://kukaniloko.org/'],['Office of Hawaiian Affairs: Wahiawā lands','https://www.oha.org/aina/wahiawa-lands/'],W('Kūkaniloko Birth Site')]),
N('diamond-head-fire-control-station','Diamond Head summit Fire Control Station','Honolulu','hi','fort','standing',21.25977,-157.81166,'site','permit','hike','forts','1908–1911',
  'The trail up Lēʻahi (Diamond Head) was built in 1908 for Oahu\'s coastal defences. At the top the Army finished a Fire Control Station in 1911, which directed the guns of the batteries in Waikiki and at Fort Ruger outside the crater.',
  'Steep stairs and a lighted 225-foot tunnel lead into the station; at the summit are concrete bunkers and a large navigation light built in 1917 (State Parks).',
  'A climb through a tunnel into a 1911 artillery lookout.',
  'Diamond Head State Monument, Hawaii Division of State Parks: open 6:00 am to 6:00 pm daily, last entry 4:30 pm; closed Christmas and New Year\'s Day. Non-residents must reserve entry and parking ahead; non-residents pay $5 entry and $10 a car, Hawaii residents with ID enter free; cards only. The summit trail starts at the lot inside the crater.',
  [SP('Diamond Head State Monument','diamond-head-state-monument')],
  th=[21.26411,-157.80477],thn='Diamond Head State Monument lot inside the crater'),
N('makapuu-point-light','Makapuʻu Point Light','Waimanalo','hi','light','standing',21.309861,-157.649743,'site','view','hike','','1909–',
  'A lighthouse on Oahu\'s eastern point, first asked for by shippers in 1888 but not funded until 1906. It was lit in 1909 with a hyperradiant Fresnel lens, about 12 feet tall with more than a thousand prisms, which Wikipedia calls the largest lens of any US lighthouse. It was automated in 1974; in 1984 a vandal shot a hole in the lens. It is listed on the National Register (1977).',
  'The short white tower below the cliff, seen from the lookout at the top of the paved Makapuʻu Point Lighthouse Trail. The lighthouse itself is off-limits.',
  'The biggest lighthouse lens in the country, seen from above.',
  'The Coast Guard keeps the light; the land round it is Kaiwi State Scenic Shoreline (Hawaii Division of State Parks). The trail is the paved former lighthouse road, 2 miles round trip and 500 feet of climb, hot and windy, with no water or restrooms; open 7:00 am to 6:45 pm in winter and to 7:45 pm in summer, no fee. The tide pools below the point are a closed and dangerous area.',
  [SP('Kaiwi State Scenic Shoreline','kaiwi-state-scenic-shoreline'),W('Makapuʻu Point Light')],
  th=[21.30583,-157.65571],thn='Makapuʻu Point Lighthouse Trail lot'),
N('diamond-head-lighthouse','Diamond Head Lighthouse','Honolulu','hi','light','standing',21.25568,-157.809492,'site','view','drive','','1899–',
  'First lit on July 1, 1899, as an iron tower inside coral rock walls, with John M. Kaukaliu as the first keeper. In 1917 the light was moved to a temporary frame while the present reinforced concrete tower was built on the old foundation. The keeper\'s house dates from 1921 and the light was automated in 1924. Since World War II the house has been the private home of the Coast Guard\'s district commander.',
  'The concrete tower and the house beside it, seen from Diamond Head Road.',
  '',
  'Coast Guard property and a private residence: not open. View from Diamond Head Road; the lookout lots along the road are the place to stop.',
  [W('Diamond Head Lighthouse')],
  th=[21.25565,-157.80767],thn='Lookout parking on Diamond Head Road'),
N('old-pali-road','Old Pali Road below the Nuʻuanu Pali Lookout','Honolulu','hi','landmark','traces',21.36713,-157.79325,'area','view','walk','','1845–1957',
  'The route over the Nuʻuanu Pali began as a steep foot trail; in 1845 it was paved with stone and widened to six feet for horses. In 1897 a carriage road was blasted into the cliff below the old trail, 20 feet wide on stone retaining walls, and opened to traffic in 1898 (HMdb markers at the lookout). It served cars with little change until the present highway and its tunnels were finished (1957 by the marker; Wikipedia says the tunnels were built in 1958). When the highway opened the old road was closed to traffic.',
  'From the lookout, the old road can be seen winding down the cliff face. Road writers describe a section with its old concrete railing near the lookout; that was not checked.',
  'The cliff road that carried windward Oahu to town before the tunnels.',
  'Nuʻuanu Pali State Wayside, Hawaii Division of State Parks: open daily 6:00 am to 6:00 pm. The old road below the lower viewing platform is gated and signed Road Closed; look at it from the lookout and do not go past the gate.',
  [SP('Nuʻuanu Pali State Wayside','nuuanu-pali-state-wayside'),['HMdb: Pali Road – From Trail to Highway','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=73554'],['HMdb: Pali Road','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=73555'],W('Nuʻuanu Pali'),W('Hawaii Route 61')],
  conf='mixed',th=[21.36671,-157.79424],thn='Nuʻuanu Pali State Wayside lot'),
N('huilua-fishpond','Huilua Fishpond','Kahana','hi','water','traces',21.557659,-157.868441,'site','public','walk','','',
  'An old Hawaiian walled fishpond at Kahana Bay. A permeable rock seawall (kuapā) closed off a body of brackish water, with sluice gates that let small fish in and kept big ones from getting out. Tsunamis in 1957 and 1960 damaged it; it was made a National Historic Landmark in 1962, and restoration by State Parks and Friends of Kahana began in 1993.',
  'The rock wall along the shore of Kahana Bay. State Parks calls it the most impressive site in the valley and says it can be visited from the east side of the bay.',
  '',
  'Ahupuaʻa ʻO Kahana State Park, Hawaii Division of State Parks: open 7:00 am to 6:45 pm in winter and to 7:45 pm in summer, no entrance fee. State Parks says to visit the pond from the east side of the bay, along Kamehameha Highway; no lot was found there on the aerial, so the parking given is the state park\'s lot on the west side of the bay.',
  [SP('Ahupuaʻa ʻO Kahana State Park','ahupuaa-o-kahana-state-park'),W('Huilua Fishpond')],
  th=[21.55533,-157.87253],thn='Kahana State Park lot by Kamehameha Highway'),
N('kapaeleele-koa-kahana','Kapaʻeleʻele Koʻa and Keaniani Kilo','Kahana','hi','burial','ruins',21.55533,-157.87253,'area','public','hike','','',
  'Kahana Valley holds many remains of old Hawaiian life: a heiau, koʻa (fishing shrines), fishponds, house sites, walled enclosures, irrigation ditches and terraces. Most are closed to the public, but the fishing shrine Kapaʻeleʻele Koʻa and Keaniani Kilo, a lookout from which fish were spotted in the bay, are on a trail at the west side of the valley mouth.',
  'The shrine and the lookout, on a one-mile loop from the park\'s Orientation Center (about an hour). Exactly where along the loop each stands was not found.',
  '',
  'Ahupuaʻa ʻO Kahana State Park, Hawaii Division of State Parks: open 7:00 am to 6:45 pm in winter and to 7:45 pm in summer, no entrance fee. The loop begins at the Orientation Center. Stay on the trail: the other sites in the valley are not open to visitors.',
  [SP('Ahupuaʻa ʻO Kahana State Park','ahupuaa-o-kahana-state-park')],
  th=[21.55533,-157.87253],thn='Kahana State Park lot by Kamehameha Highway'),
N('heeia-fishpond','Heʻeia Fishpond','Kaneohe','hi','water','traces',21.43504,-157.80521,'site','view','drive','','',
  'A walled coastal fishpond in Kāneʻohe Bay, the only Hawaiian fishpond whose wall runs all the way round it, on the seaward and landward sides. The wall is dry-laid basalt with a coral fill and has seven sluice gates. Photographs from 1880 to 1910 show it well kept; invasive mangrove arrived about 1922 and development crowded it from the 1930s to the 1960s. Restoration began in 1988; the nonprofit Paepae o Heʻeia has cared for it since 2001.',
  'The ring of stone wall in the bay, seen from Heʻeia State Park, which State Parks describes as having good views of the fishpond.',
  '',
  'The pond is cared for by Paepae o Heʻeia under an agreement with Kamehameha Schools; visits are by its walking tours and community workdays only (ask through paepaeoheeia.org). Heʻeia State Park, on the point to the north, is open daily 8:00 am to 5:00 pm with no fee.',
  [['Paepae o Heʻeia','https://paepaeoheeia.org/'],SP('Heʻeia State Park','heeia-state-park'),W('Heʻeia Fishpond')],
  th=[21.44204,-157.80851],thn='Heʻeia State Park, by the hall on Kealohi Point'),
N('koko-crater-tramway','Koko Crater Tramway','Honolulu','hi','rail','traces',21.28203,-157.69034,'site','public','hike','inclines','1942–1966',
  'During World War II the Army laid a cable tramway straight up the side of Koko Crater (Kohelepelepe) to carry men and supplies to bunkers on the summit, which served as a radar station. The military gave the site to the City and County of Honolulu in 1966, and the old track became a hiking trail.',
  'More than 1,000 steps of old tramway ties climbing the crater, a wooden trestle part way up, and the former military bunkers at the top. The city sealed the bunker openings in 2025 after a fall; stay on the tramway.',
  'A wartime cable railway that is now Honolulu\'s hardest staircase.',
  'Koko Head District Park, City and County of Honolulu Department of Parks and Recreation. Park in the district park lots off Koko Head Park Road. The city reopened the tramway on July 10, 2025 and asks hikers to stay on it and heed the warning signs and tape at the summit. Hours not checked.',
  [['City of Honolulu Parks: Koko Crater Tramway reopened','https://www.honolulu.gov/dpr/dpr-news/koko-crater-tramway-remains-closed-pending-additional-bunker-sealing/'],W('Koko Crater')],
  th=[21.27779,-157.69298],thn='Koko Head District Park lot'),
N('kamananui-valley-road','Kamananui Valley Road and its stone bridges','Honolulu','hi','landmark','traces',21.38048,-157.86291,'area','public','hike','','1890s',
  'Moanalua (Kamananui) Valley belonged to the Damon family, who built a carriage road up it. The Star-Bulletin wrote in 2007 that its seven stone bridges were built by the Damon Estate in the 1890s, hand-crafted by Italian masons. That year the state bought 3,716 acres of the valley from the Samuel Mills Damon Estate, and in 2009 it opened the 2.5-mile road to the public as a state forest trail.',
  'A dirt road along the stream with stretches of old cobblestone, seven arched stone bridges, a short loop to the May Damon house site where an old brick chimney remains, and, at the seventh bridge, the petroglyph boulder Pōhaku Luahine (a 2008 Honolulu Advertiser hike guide; not checked on the ground).',
  '',
  'State land (Honolulu Watershed Forest Reserve, Moanalua section). The trail starts at the back of the city\'s Moanalua Valley Neighborhood Park, at the end of Ala Aolani Street. Hours not checked. The pin is a point on the road, not a particular bridge.',
  [['Honolulu Advertiser (2009): road opened to the public','https://archives-new.honoluluadvertiser.com/article/2009/Jun/06/il/hawaii306060001.html'],['Honolulu Advertiser (2008): Moanalua Valley hike','https://archives-new.honoluluadvertiser.com/article/2008/Sep/07/tr/hawaii809070339.html'],['Star-Bulletin (2007): bridges built by the Damon Estate','https://archives.starbulletin.com/2007/04/02/news/story07.html']],
  conf='mixed',th=[21.37403,-157.88053],thn='Moanalua Valley Neighborhood Park lot'),
N('kaena-point-railroad','Oahu Railway grade at Kaʻena Point','Waialua','hi','rail','traces',21.57932,-158.23724,'area','public','hike','','1890s–1946',
  'The Oahu Railway and Land Company ran its line round Kaʻena Point, the island\'s western tip, to carry sugar cane. Wikipedia\'s railway article says the line was carried round the point to Haleiwa by June 1897; its Kaʻena Point article says the railway there was built in 1899. Most of the track was destroyed by the 1946 tsunami and the company was dissolved in 1947.',
  'Parts of the old rail bed are visible along the Kaʻena Point Trail. Where exactly was not checked.',
  'Walk the old sugar railway round the tip of Oahu.',
  'Kaʻena Point State Park, Hawaii Division of State Parks: open daily 6:00 am to 7:00 pm. From the Mokuleʻia side, park at the gravel lot where the paved end of Farrington Highway stops; driving beyond the gate needs a vehicle permit. No dogs, drones or camping; no water, and no restrooms on the Mokuleʻia side. The point is about 2.5 miles each way. In November 2026 the Keawaʻula (west side) trail was still closed after storm damage; check the State Parks page before going.',
  [SP('Kaʻena Point State Park','kaena-point-state-park'),W('Kaʻena Point, Oʻahu'),W('Oahu Railway and Land Company')],
  conf='mixed',th=[21.57932,-158.23724],thn='Kaʻena Point State Park, Mokuleʻia gravel lot'),
N('royal-mausoleum-mauna-ala','Royal Mausoleum (Mauna ʻAla)','Honolulu','hi','burial','standing',21.325,-157.847222,'site','public','drive','cemeteries','1865–',
  'When the royal tomb near ʻIolani Palace was full, a new mausoleum was built in Nuʻuanu Valley under Bishop Thomas Staley. Its west wing was ready in 1864 and it was finished in 1865, when the remains of earlier royals were carried here by torchlight on October 30. In 1887 the Kamehameha family\'s caskets were moved to an underground vault built for Charles Reed Bishop, and the Territory later built a second crypt.',
  'Tombs of the Kamehameha and Kalākaua families and their retainers, a historic chapel, old trees and the curator\'s cottage (State Parks).',
  '',
  'Royal Mausoleum State Monument, Hawaii Division of State Parks: open Monday to Friday 8:00 am to 4:30 pm, closed weekends and holidays, and closed for repairs on November 27, 2026. Groups of more than 25 need a permit; smaller groups are asked to contact the curator. Some days are reserved for ceremonies. This is a burial ground: behave as at a grave.',
  [SP('Royal Mausoleum State Monument','royal-mausoleum-state-monument'),W('Royal Mausoleum (Mauna ʻAla)')]),
N('battery-randolph-army-museum','Battery Randolph (Army Museum of Hawaii)','Honolulu','hi','fort','standing',21.27879,-157.83330,'site','public','walk','forts','',
  'A coast artillery battery at Fort DeRussy in Waikiki that mounted two 14-inch disappearing guns, the first line of defence against a naval attack on Oahu\'s south shore. The 14-inch guns were scrapped after World War II, and the battery became the Army Museum of Hawaii in 1976. When it was built was not taken from a source opened here.',
  'The massive concrete battery itself, with museum exhibits inside on the military history of Hawaii. Wikipedia says two 7-inch naval guns from the battleship USS New Hampshire now stand in place of the old guns.',
  '',
  'US Army, Fort DeRussy, 2131 Kalia Road at Saratoga Road. Free; open 10 am to 5 pm Tuesday to Saturday, closed Sunday and Monday, and closed the Tuesday after a Monday federal holiday (US Army page). Wikipedia says parking is across the street.',
  th=[21.28092,-157.83176],thn='Lot on Saratoga Road across from the museum',links=[['US Army Garrison Hawaii: Army Museum','https://home.army.mil/hawaii/army-museum'],W('U.S. Army Museum of Hawaii')]),
]
PIN_NOTES={'puu-o-mahuka-heiau': 'National Register list coordinate. The aerial shows the pin inside the big walled stone enclosure; the lot is the clearing at its east end. LiDAR sheet shows the platform edges.',
'keaiwa-heiau': 'From the OpenStreetMap building mapped as Keaiwa Heiau (historic=ruins). The Register list point was 400 m north in forest on private watershed land; the aerial at the new pin shows a walled rectangular enclosure inside the state recreation area, at the park entry as State Parks describes.',
'ulupo-heiau': 'National Register list coordinate (the ArcGIS geocoder gives the same point). The aerial shows the pin at the south edge of the large dark terraced platform, beside the YMCA.',
'kukaniloko-birthstones': 'National Register list coordinate. The aerial shows a ring of stones inside a grove at the centre of a walled enclosure in the field at Whitmore Avenue and Kamehameha Highway; the federal land map names the parcel Kukaniloko Birthstones State Monument.',
'diamond-head-fire-control-station': 'From the OpenStreetMap building "Fire Control Station Diamond Head" (military=bunker). The aerial shows the summit structure on the crater rim at the end of the summit trail. Parking is the State Monument lot inside the crater.',
'makapuu-point-light': 'National Register list coordinate. The aerial shows the ring on the small white tower on the cliff below the point. Parking is the trail lot at the bottom of the old lighthouse road.',
'diamond-head-lighthouse': 'National Register list coordinate. The aerial shows the pin in the walled lighthouse compound on the sea side of Diamond Head Road; the tower is not clearly told apart from the house at this scale. Parking is the lookout shoulder to the east.',
'old-pali-road': 'The Nuʻuanu Pali Lookout point from the ArcGIS geocoder, which matches the HMdb marker position (21° 22.028′ N, 157° 47.595′ W). The aerial shows the lookout platform; the old road itself winds below it and is not pinned. No 1 m LiDAR here.',
'huilua-fishpond': 'National Register list coordinate (OpenStreetMap maps the pond at the same spot). The pin is in the pond; the aerial and the LiDAR both show the stone wall along its west and north sides.',
'kapaeleele-koa-kahana': 'Pin is the Kahana State Park lot by the highway, mapped in OpenStreetMap (tools/parking.py) and seen on the aerial as a lot by a building at the back of the beach. The shrine and the lookout are on the one-mile loop from the Orientation Center; their exact spots were not found, so the pin marks only the start.',
'heeia-fishpond': 'The Wikipedia coordinate fell on houses on the shore. Moved with tools/pix.py onto the east (seaward) wall of the ring-walled pond, which the aerial shows plainly. Parking is a loop drive in Heʻeia State Park on the point to the north.',
'koko-crater-tramway': 'From the OpenStreetMap steps "Koko Crater Tramway". The aerial shows the ring on the straight tramway line climbing the crater; the LiDAR shows the cut. Parking is the district park lot at the foot.',
'kamananui-valley-road': 'From the OpenStreetMap footway "Kamananui Valley Road", about 2 km up the valley. The aerial shows only forest; the LiDAR shows the road bed curving through the pin. No bridge or other feature is pinned. Parking is the neighbourhood park lot at the trailhead.',
'kaena-point-railroad': 'Pin is the Mokuleʻia gravel lot at the end of Farrington Highway (OpenStreetMap parking, seen on the aerial as a dirt lot by the shore), not the rail bed: where the grade shows along the trail was not found. No 1 m LiDAR here.',
'royal-mausoleum-mauna-ala': 'National Register list coordinate; OpenStreetMap maps the cemetery at the same spot. The aerial shows the pin in the grounds among the tombs and the chapel.',
'battery-randolph-army-museum': 'From the OpenStreetMap building "United States Army Museum of Hawaii" (old_name Battery Randolph), which agrees with the ArcGIS geocoder for 2131 Kalia Road. The aerial shows the spot beside a hotel tower, in its shadow; the Register list point was 450 m away. Parking moved with tools/pix.py onto the stalls of the Saratoga Road lot.'}
